Miles Brundage raises concerns about the US government's approach to export controls on advanced AI models and chips related to China. He questions why controls have been loosened or not enforced, allowing China to access AI chips, training capabilities, and semiconductor manufacturing equipment, and why restrictions are not applied uniformly to all models and countries.

He argues that if the administration is serious about preventing China from building or obtaining advanced AI models, it should implement a comprehensive strategy addressing these loopholes. Brundage points out that recent actions have instead opened new loopholes and relaxed existing controls, suggesting a lack of genuine effort to curb China's access to advanced AI technology.

@ReedAlbergotti reports that the White House's export limits on Anthropic are linked to concerns about Chinese access to Mythos, though Trump adviser David Sacks claims restrictions are unrelated to conflicts with the AI company.

